  "label": "Metapopulation Models For Pathogen Surveillance",
  "related": [
    "transmission-model-5722",
    "urban-borne-7576",
    "app-reproducible-69d3"
  ],
  "secondary_categories": [],
  "slug": "laws-masked-5b6d",
  "summary": "Recent metapopulation studies of COVID-19, polio and malaria treat mobility and observation processes as explicit model components.",
  "synthesis": "The dominant thread in these three studies is treating population structure and observation processes as explicit model components rather than fixed background. An age-structured SEIR metapopulation model of COVID-19 in Seoul, using mobile-phone mobility data, finds that targeting adult work flows within clusters reduces transmission. A statistical analysis estimates time-varying polio surveillance sensitivity for each Nigerian LGA and validates it against 2014-2020 WPV1 absence periods. A stochastic metapopulation model of malaria in western Kenya jointly incorporates climate, interventions, immunity and mobility over 2008-2019. The methods differ across studies, so this describes a shared direction rather than a uniform one. What is new is the joint treatment of mobility structure and surveillance performance as analytic targets, although only the polio study estimates surveillance performance directly.",
  "synthesis_model": "claude-haiku-5-5",
  "synthesized_on": "2026-03-22"
}
